>Well, it says that David over prepares for the late game.

Not really. I do, but that isn't really what makes Asmodeus a wuss. I
don't do two-weapon combat; I use the best artifact weapon readily
available enchanted to +6 or +7. You don't even need to wish for a magic
marker and write SoEW to get one weapon to +6. So he's not running out of
hitpoints because I'm hitting him with an unexpectedly powerful attack.

Asmodeus has an AC of -7. I do not need to take any special steps to
improve my chance to hit to have a 100% chance of hitting him.

Asmodeus does cold damage. Having cold resistance by Gehennom is not, I
think, generally considered overpreparation. Given the frequency with
which things like yeti show up, you'd have to work at it _not_ to have
cold resistance.

Asmodeus does 4d4 with his claw attack. This averages 10 points. Negative
AC absorbs damage equal to a random number of points between 1 and the
negative AC. I hardly need to get into things like draining +4 armour to
retry for +5 (which I don't do) to cause almost all his melee attacks to
inflict insignificant damage; simply having a DSM, armour from the
dungeon, and donating readily available gold will make his attack
meaningless.

How do I overprepare? Lots of curse removal - but Asmodeus can't curse
anything. Lots of PoFH and hitpoints - but Asmodeus can't do significant
damage. A method to map Gehennom - Asmodeus is that method. Free action -
Asmodeus can't paralyse me. Digging wands to escape summoning storms - but
he can only summon one demon at a time. Face it, he's just a wuss.
